{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Live corroboration: a unit-cost alarm has now fired on the primary model family\n\nAt 2026-09-18T00:45 local, CloudWatch alarm `bedrock-unit-cost-high-sonnet-4-6` transitioned\nto ALARM on three consecutive hourly datapoints (04:45, 05:45, 06:45 UTC), each exceeding its\nconfigured threshold. `EvaluationPeriods` is 3 and `TreatMissingData` is `notBreaching`, so\nthis was a sustained crossing on real datapoints, not a missing-data artifact.\n\nThis is the failure mode this ticket predicts, observed in production. The alarm is scoped to\n`sonnet-4-6` — the model family the rate card cannot price. Since every invocation for that\nfamily takes the fail-closed fallback rather than a real rate, the unit-cost series the alarm\nevaluates cannot be a measurement of anything. It is the synthetic fallback figure, and the\nalarm is now reacting to it as though it were observed cost.\n\nTwo consequences worth recording:\n\n1. **The alarm is unactionable in its current form.** An operator paged by it cannot tell\n   whether unit cost genuinely moved or whether the fallback constant simply sits above the\n   threshold. Until this ticket is fixed, the alarm's state carries no information about\n   actual spend, so it is noise that will train operators to ignore a cost signal.\n\n2. **This is the second independent consumer of the broken rate card**, alongside the two\n   thresholds already named in the description. The scope of the defect is therefore wider\n   than the spend cap itself: anything downstream of `rates` in SSM `/bedrock-spend-cap/state`\n   inherits the synthetic number.\n\n### Confirmed still broken today\n\nRead from `/bedrock-spend-cap/state` during this tick:\n\n- `rates_day` is `2026-09-18` — the card refreshed today, so this is not a stale-card problem.\n- The refreshed card still contains **zero entries for any Anthropic Claude model**. The\n  families present are Nova, Llama, DeepSeek, Mistral, Pixtral, gpt-oss, minimax, and Titan\n  embeddings.\n- `ce_spend` is `0.0` with `ce_last_poll` at `2026-09-18T07:36:20Z` — a fresh poll returning\n  a structurally zero figure.\n\nSo the daily refresh is running and succeeding, and reproducing the same omission each day.\nWhatever populates this card is not merely stale; it has never included the primary model\nfamily, and a successful refresh does not repair it.\n\n### Operational impact right now\n\nAn autonomous delivery loop is currently configured to consult this meter as its spend guard\nbefore dispatching billable work. With `ce_spend` structurally zero, that guard cannot fire on\nreal spend — it can only ever fire on the daily-warning path, which this ticket already\nidentifies as firing on a synthetic number. The guard is therefore not currently protective in\neither direction: it will not stop work when it should, and when it does stop work the reason\nwill not correspond to measured spend.\n\nThis raises the practical urgency of the fix beyond reporting accuracy.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789717745287082487,"uuid":"7358ac7d-f584-409c-8dce-5b3da3a1a041"}